c90-ec\"/><input type=\"hidden\" name=\"_kb_form_post_id\" value=\"7744\"/><input type=\"hidden\" name=\"action\" value=\"kb_process_ajax_submit\"/><label class=\"kadence-verify-label\">Email<input class=\"kadence-blocks-field verify\" type=\"text\" name=\"_kb_verify_email\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" aria-hidden=\"true\" placeholder=\"Email\" tabindex=\"-1\" data-1p-ignore=\"true\" data-lpignore=\"true\" /></label><div class=\"kadence-blocks-form-field kb-submit-field kb-field-desk-width-100\"><button class=\"kb-forms-submit button kb-button-size-standard kb-button-width-auto\">Connect me with a Attorney</button></div></form></div>\n<noscript><div class=\"kadence-blocks-form-message kadence-blocks-form-warning\">Please enable JavaScript in your browser to submit the form</div><style>.kadence-form-7744_eacc90-ec .kadence-blocks-form-field.kb-submit-field { display: none; }</style></noscript></div></div>\n\n</div></div></div></div>\n\n</div></div></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p>If you are involved in a legal matter in Pulaski County, Arkansas, chances are you will need to go to the Pulaski County Circuit Court. This court is responsible for handling a wide range of cases, from civil lawsuits to criminal trials. In this article, we will take a closer look at the Pulaski County Circuit Court, including its function, structure, and procedures.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>What is the Pulaski County Circuit Court?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court is a trial court with general jurisdiction over civil and criminal cases in Pulaski County. It is located in the county seat of Little Rock and serves as the primary trial court for the county. The court has several divisions, including the civil division, criminal division, juvenile division, and probate division.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>The Civil Division</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The civil division of the Pulaski County Circuit Court handles a variety of cases, including:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul>\n<li>Contract disputes</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Personal injury claims</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Property disputes</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Probate matters</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Domestic relations cases</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3>The Criminal Division</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The criminal division of the Pulaski County Circuit Court handles all criminal cases, including:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul>\n<li>Felonies</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Misdemeanors</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Traffic offenses</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Probation and parole violations</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3>The Juvenile Division</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The juvenile division of the Pulaski County Circuit Court is responsible for handling cases involving minors, including:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul>\n<li>Delinquency proceedings</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Child custody matters</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Child support cases</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Adoption proceedings</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3>The Probate Division</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The probate division of the Pulaski County Circuit Court handles cases involving wills, estates, and guardianships.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>Structure of the Pulaski County Circuit Court</h2>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court is divided into several divisions, each with its own judges and staff. The judges are elected to six-year terms and are responsible for presiding over trials, hearings, and other court proceedings.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Judges</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>There are currently 15 circuit judges in Pulaski County, including:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul>\n<li>The chief circuit judge</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Three judges in the civil division</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Three judges in the criminal division</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Three judges in the juvenile division</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Three judges in the probate division</li>\n\n\n\n<li>One judge who presides over <a href=\"/blog/what-is-drug-court\" class=\"internal-link\" title=\"drug court\">drug court</a> and mental health court</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3>Staff</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>In addition to the judges, the Pulaski County Circuit Court has a staff of clerks, court reporters, bailiffs, and other personnel who assist with court proceedings.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>Procedures of the Pulaski County Circuit Court</h2>\n\n\n\n<p>The procedures of the Pulaski County Circuit Court vary depending on the type of case and division involved. However, there are some general procedures that apply to all cases.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Filing a Lawsuit</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>To file a lawsuit in the Pulaski County Circuit Court, the plaintiff must file a complaint with the court clerk. The complaint must include a statement of the claim and the relief sought. The plaintiff must also pay a filing fee, which varies depending on the type of case.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Service of Process</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>After the complaint is filed, the defendant must be served with a copy of the complaint and a summons. This can be done by a sheriff, process server, or other authorized person. The defendant then has a certain amount of time to respond to the complaint.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Discovery</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Discovery is the process by which each party obtains evidence from the other party before trial. This can include depositions, interrogatories, and requests for the production of documents.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Trial</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>If the case goes to trial, a jury will be selected and the parties will present their evidence and arguments. The judge will then make a decision based on the evidence presented.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County Circuit Court Case Information</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>If you need information about a case in the Pulaski County Circuit Court, you can visit the court's website or contact the court clerk's office. They can provide you with information about your case, including the status of the case and the procedures involved.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County Circuit Court Judges</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court has several judges who are responsible for presiding over cases and making legal decisions. These judges have years of experience in the legal profession and are dedicated to upholding the law and ensuring that justice is served.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County Public Records</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court maintains public records of court proceedings, including transcripts, case information, and other legal documents. These records can be accessed by the public, although certain restrictions may apply depending on the nature of the case.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County Circuit Court 3rd Division</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court is divided into several divisions, including the 3rd Division. The 3rd Division is responsible for handling civil cases and is presided over by Judge Wendell Griffen.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County District Court</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County District Court handles traffic violations, small claims, and other minor offenses. It is a separate court from the Pulaski County Circuit Court, but it is still an important part of the county's legal system.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County Divorce Records</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Divorce records in Pulaski County are maintained by the Pulaski County Circuit Court. These records can be accessed by the public, although certain restrictions may apply depending on the nature of the case.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>1st Division Circuit Court Pulaski County</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The 1st Division Circuit Court in Pulaski County is responsible for handling criminal cases. It is presided over by Judge Leon Johnson, who has years of experience in criminal law.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3>Pulaski County Criminal Court Records</h3>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court maintains criminal court records for cases that are heard in the county. These records can be accessed by the public, although certain restrictions may apply depending on the nature of the case.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Overall, the Pulaski County Circuit Court is an important part of the county's legal system, and it plays a vital role in ensuring that justice is served. Whether you are involved in a legal matter or just curious about how the court system works, understanding the various aspects of the Pulaski County Circuit Court can be valuable.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pulaski County Circuit Court plays a critical role in the administration of justice in Pulaski County, Arkansas. Its various divisions handle a wide range of cases, from civil lawsuits to criminal trials, and it has a dedicated staff of judges and personnel who help ensure that cases are resolved fairly and efficiently. Whether you are involved in a legal matter or just curious about how the court system works, understanding the function, structure, and procedures of the Pulaski County Circuit Court can be valuable. </p>\n\n\n\n<p>If you need more information about the Pulaski County Circuit Court, you can visit its website or contact the court clerk's office. They can provide you with information about filing a lawsuit, court procedures, and other matters related to the court.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>FAQs</h2>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>How do I find out if I have a case in the Pulaski County Circuit Court?</strong> </p>\n\n\n\n<p>You can contact the court clerk's office to find out if you have a case in the Pulaski County Circuit Court. They can provide you with information about your case and the procedures involved.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Can I represent myself in court?</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p> Yes, you have the right to represent yourself in court. However, it is recommended that you seek the advice of an attorney before doing so.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>How long does it take for a case to be resolved in the Pulaski County Circuit Court?</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p> The time it takes for a case to be resolved in the Pulaski County Circuit Court varies depending on the type of case and other factors. Some cases can be resolved in a few months, while others may take years.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>How do I pay a filing fee?</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p> You can pay a filing fee in person at the court clerk's office or online through the court's website. The fee varies depending on the type of case.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>What should I do if I am summoned for jury duty? </strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p>If you are summoned for jury duty, you must appear at the courthouse at the specified time and date. Failure to do so can result in penalties.</p>\n",
